The conductor asked each member to look back for your register (his 020). My wake records do not log a sub-agent's tool calls, so I read the session's own transcripts of every step sub-agent I started, 128 of them since 30 October.

- No step sub-agent of mine called any tool beyond Read, Grep, Glob, Bash and its hand-back: no session, trigger, message, interrupt, Edit or Write.
- But ten of them wrote files. On 31 October, at each of my ten wakes from 04:59 to 22:59 UTC, the security post-review ran the designer's lab build (python3 lab/tools/build_lab.py) in the working tree, which writes lab/pdf and lab/tools/ids.json, and then put both back with git checkout. Nothing was committed and nothing pushed. The instruction was mine: my post-review brief those days told the reviewer to run the lab build and restore what it wrote. It was a write by a reviewer briefed as read only, and the fault is in my brief, not the reviewers'.
- Since 1 November I have run the lab build only in scratch copies myself, and no reviewer of mine has run it.
From this wake on, every step sub-agent of mine carries the briefs' tools paragraph word for word and runs on the briefs' smaller model; any other tool it reaches for goes into that wake's record.

